  <related-activity ref="XM-DAC-41114-PROJECT-00105607" type="1"></related-activity>
  <conditions attached="1">
   <condition type="1">
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Consistent with the Article III of the SBAA [or the Supplemental Provisions to the Project Document], the responsibility for the safety and security of the Implementing Partner and its personnel and property, and of UNDP&#x2019;s property in the Implementing Partner&#x2019;s custody, rests with the Implementing Partner.  To this end, the Implementing Partner shall: a)	put in place an appropriate security plan and maintain the security plan, taking into account the security situation in the country where the project is being carried; b)	assume all risks and liabilities related to the Implementing Partner&#x2019;s security, and the full implementation of the security plan.</narrative>
   </condition>
   <condition type="1">
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Social and environmental sustainability will be enhanced through application of the UNDP Social and Environmental Standards (http://www.undp.org/ses) and related Accountability Mechanism (http://www.undp.org/secu-srm).</narrative>
   </condition>
  </conditions>
  <result aggregation-status="1" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Annual Output Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator ascending="0" measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output Indicator</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">- Number of new partnership mechanisms with funding for sustainable management solutions of natural resources, ecosystem services, chemicals and waste at national and/or sub-national level.
- Number of new jobs created through solutions for management of natural resources, ecosystem services, chemicals and waste.
- Pure PCBs and of PCB- contaminated equipment safely managed and disposed or decontaminated by the end of the project, reducing worker exposure and the potential for unintentional release of these materials into the environment.</narrative>
    </description>
    <baseline year="2018">
     <comment>
      <narrative xml:lang="EN">No public private partnership exists in Nigeria dedicated to the management and disposal of PCBs waste in Nigeria. 
People and workers are currently exposed to the risk posed by PCB-containing equipment stored or in-service.  PCB contaminated oil is being used, burnt or disposed without the proper care that it is required. No Financial resources are allocated to properly manage and dispose of PCB containing wastes.</narrative>
     </comment>
    </baseline>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2021-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2021-12-31"></period-end>
     <target>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">(i). Training for high level officers and decision makers on mainstreaming of PCBs and general POPs issues into development plans.
(ii) Upgrade and regular update of the project website and social media platforms for continuous improvement in content delivery, visibility and accessibility
(iii) Carry out a national PCB inventory in 22 states not covered by previous inventory (six consultants, activities to run concurrently)
(iv)Update PCBs Management Information System (MIS)
(v). Sample and analyse at least 11,000 samples (Test kits onsite use and lab analyses for quantitative analyses) -quantitative analyses at BCRCC-Africa Ibadan, UNILAG and SHETCO</narrative>
      </comment>
     </target>
     <actual>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">(i) Inventories for the 22 States initiated
(ii) MIS database completed
(iii) Capacity development of relevant staff of stakeholder institutions completed</narrative>
      </comment>
     </actual>
    </period>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2020-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2020-12-31"></period-end>
     <target>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">(i) Printing of regulations and guidelines document.
(ii) Gender Dimension in the context of PCBs issue in Nigeria, strategies for better Gender Mainstreaming in POPs related activities identified
(iii) Carry out a national PCB inventory in all states not previously covered in Phase 1 according to project document
(iv) Procurement of PCB analytical equipment and consumables for SHESTCO laboratory</narrative>
      </comment>
     </target>
     <actual>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">(i) PCBs ESM Regulations and Guidelines have been developed. The regulations are being considered for approval by Federal Ministry of Justice (FMoJ). Thereafter the two documents are to be printed and circulated.
(ii). The study to investigate the effects of PCBs in women has been captured in AWP-2020, and would be conducted in the third quarter of the year. The assessment has been approved by the Project Steering Committee.
(iii). Consultants have been engaged for the inventory activities in 22 states not previously covered. However, due to challenges of COVID-19, field PCBs inventory is yet to take off.
(iv). Process for the Equipment (GC) for sampling and testing was launched.</narrative>
